	I call the first of the three cases I will present the "generational silo" case. It reflects some of the characteristics of the real progression of language loss as it actually occurs, with some artificialities, for the sake of theoretical tractability.
	Imagine a hypothetical community in which we carve out two distinct generations by appeal to an exact age line, and each group on either side speaks a different first language. For the moment, then, we will be working with a non-intuitive, rarified idea of what a generation is, as it means dividing the entire population of this community by age into only two groups and calling each group a "generation". For the current purpose of the hypothetical, it does not matter much what this age line is exactly, but generally, the further towards the end of average life expectancy such a line is drawn, the later the stage of language endangerment that this reflects, although of course real-life cases will involve more delineations, layers, and vague boundaries under which multiple generations are defined. Let us suppose here the dividing line is age 40, so the younger generation consists of those aged 0-39 years and the older generation consists of those aged 40-100+ years. Even though it does not quite map on to our ordinary usage of the terms, out of convenience I will refer to the members of the older generation as the elders, and those of the younger generation as the youth, given that any two members picked from each of the differing groups will be older and younger relative to one another.
	Now, assume that the elders and the youth are also divided just as exactly into two separate language groups, so that each generation grew up speaking a different first language. Call the first language of the older generation L1x and the first language of the younger generation L1y. Let us also stipulate that only the elders speak L1x and only the youth speak L1y, and we take this to mean that no one in the community is fluent in both L1x and L1y. The elders will be the last ones to speak L1x if the younger generation does not learn it. But suppose in this scenario that none of the younger generation is learning L1x. Further, suppose there is another language L2 that both generations have in common, which allows them to communicate across the generational line, but which is neither generation's first language.
	Such a situation may be similar to what the objector in the generational transfer objection was imagining, insofar as it involves different generations becoming silo'd off from one another linguistically. Each generation is confined to speaking their first language amongst only themselves. It is already a question whether or not such a situation is one in which any individual member counts as being cut off from their first language community, by Nowak's lights. In one sense it would seem not to be so; there are other members of the community with whom any member may continue to speak their first language. All things considered, no individual finds herself in a situation of completely losing access to her first language community.
	At the same time, even though there exists a schism in the two generations where their first language is concerned, we have stipulated that there is a mutual second language (L2) by which they are able to communicate intergenerationally. Though all of the losses that occur when speaking a second language as opposed to one's first are then incurred by any members when communicating in the L2, they are temporary so long as one stays within reach of those who speak their respective L1.
	As opposed to the situation discussed in the generational transfer case, there are not any members of the community who are fully or even partially bilingual in either L1x or L1y to begin with. In this case, then, no one from the younger generation will suffer a first-language loss when L1x is no longer spoken after the elders pass.
	It appears then, that the situation as it is described is not a defective one for either generation, judging by the standards set forth from a very strict interpretation of Nowak's view. Since each generation constitutes its own first-language community, no individual is deprived of the autonomy to craft her social identity with the tools found only in her first language. But it is still clear that this is a scenario in which a language, L1x, stands to disappear completely with enough time. 
	One clear way forward for Nowak's view is to observe here that as time passes and as members from the elder generation pass away, this will result in the first-language community for the elders shrinking over time. With none of the youth learning L1x, there are no younger speakers to replenish the L1x speakers who pass away in the elder generation. Hence, the elders are left with fewer and fewer people with whom they can speak L1x over time. Though Nowak does not himself spell out constitutes being cut off from one's linguistic community, I think this can be admitted as one very real way in which such a thing happens. It is not always from the explicit forbidding of speaking certain languages or of sudden displacement of members away from their home territories that people become cut off from their first language communities- the shrinking of these communities themselves over time is another salient sense in which individuals may experience a sort of "cutting off." Insofar as Nowak's view succeeds in showing that people ought not to be cut off from their linguistic communities, then it seems the mere observation of what the future trajectory of L1x looks like for the elders is enough to generate a concern for preserving the language.
	Even still, given the present constraints of our hypothetical scenario, I think that matters are worse for Nowak's view than this, as it stands. For, in some sense there is a symmetry of concerns. There is no reason related to autonomy over one's own social identity that any member has to learn the first language of the other generation. Worse than this, considerations of autonomy, of the kind Nowak develops, seem to indicate an interest each generation has in continuing to speak the first language that they already do, and *not* to learn the first language of the other. Each generation will face the loss of their first language if they fully adopt the first language of the other, and given that there is already a common L2 that enables intergenerational communication, there does not seem to be much reason to move toward L1x-L1y bilingualism in the youth without a built-in regard for the fact that L1x faces disappearance otherwise.
	Not only this, but given that a good amount of the youth are past the window in which they could come to aquire full bilingual command over L1y and L1x, there is a good chunk of the younger generation which likely cannot really become part of the elders' first-language community anyway, at least not in the sense in which they would count as full-fledged first-language speakers who can meet the elders' L1x fluency in kind. Again, on a strict interpretation of Nowak's view, it is this kind of first-language fluency that presumably must be reciprocated in an L1x first-speaker's conversational partner if she is to cash in on the favored sense of autonomy over controlling the meaning of her social identity. Without the possibility of even becoming conversational partners that could enable the elders to reap this good, what reason is there for any member of the youth to learn L1x when there is already L2 that likely offers the same or even more effective means of commmunication?
	That is to say that I think the considerations Nowak develops for why individual speakers should retain access to their first-language communities can, to an extent, offer some diagnosis of what exactly is wrong with the hypothetical situation we're considering now. That the scenario is one wherein certain individuals will over time find access to other first-language speakers fewer and farther between, and that this means the opportunity to be seen and understood as they can only be in their first language will diminish proportionally, gives us an explanation as to what is bad about L1x's trajectory. 
	The heart of the matter that I am driving at is that Nowak's view can explain perfectly well why. the given hypothetical situation should not come to pass. If everything goes smoothly... but once it does happen, the considerations meant to preent language decline often won't do the trick in motivation a restoration of a language...
	But the revitalizationist attitude is that ideally the younger generation should be learning L1x, because otherwise L1x will eventually fall out of use. Such a worry characterizes early stages of language endangerment. There is no built-in regard for the status of a vulnerable language. But this is precisely what is needed to motivate special efforts to ensure its future.
~[Upshot: expected view] Even in such an abstract case, it seems intuitive that from a 	revitalizationist or preservationist perspective, the situation is not ideal. L1x is a waning language, and thus the inclination would be to intervene with efforts toward language documentation and preservation, at the least. The true revitalizationist impulse would be to stimulate acquisition of L1x among the younger generation.

